 \begin{abstract}
-Autonomous unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) deployed in wilderness search-and-rescue (SAR) missions require reliable person detection under severe size, weight, power, and computing (SWaP-C) constraints. While multimodal sensing combining visible red-green-blue (RGB) and thermal infrared (TIR) cameras mitigates single-modality dropouts caused by canopy shade, darkness, or thermal crossover, frame-level object detectors frequently produce transient false positive spikes. In autonomous aerial operations, each false alarm triggers wasteful loiter maneuvers, battery depletion, and spurious transmissions over bandwidth-constrained Internet of Things (IoT) satellite or cellular links. This paper presents a systematic comparative evaluation of causal temporal post-processing methods designed to convert continuous multimodal confidence streams into reliable alarm decisions while suppressing false alarms. We deploy the lightweight YOLO11n detector (2.6 million parameters, 6.5 GFLOPs at $640 \times 640$ resolution) trained with batch size 16 in single-precision floating-point (FP32) arithmetic to guarantee numerical stability on embedded avionics. Frame confidences are integrated via a soft disjunctive late fusion gate and filtered across a causal five-frame sliding window ($625\text{ ms}$ latency bound at $8\text{ Hz}$). We evaluate five post-processing strategies: baseline raw thresholding, five-frame moving average, five-frame median filtering, five-frame history consensus, and a learned pure-PyTorch Mamba selective state-space model (SSSM), with decision thresholds optimized systematically on validation sequences. Evaluations across synthetic SAR video benchmarks spanning desert, forest, and snow terrains demonstrate that causal temporal filtering effectively suppresses environmental false alarms while preserving target sensitivity.
+Autonomous unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) deployed in wilderness search-and-rescue (SAR) missions require reliable person detection under severe size, weight, power, and computing (SWaP-C) constraints. While multimodal sensing combining visible red-green-blue (RGB) and thermal infrared (TIR) cameras mitigates single-modality dropouts caused by canopy shade, darkness, or thermal crossover, frame-level object detectors frequently produce transient false positive spikes. In autonomous aerial operations, each false alarm triggers wasteful loiter maneuvers, battery depletion, and spurious transmissions over bandwidth-constrained Internet of Things (IoT) satellite or cellular links. This paper presents a systematic comparative evaluation of causal temporal post-processing methods designed to convert continuous confidence streams into reliable alarm decisions while suppressing false alarms across modalities and environments. We freeze our study around two wilderness environments (arid desert and temperate forest) and two visual modalities (visible RGB and thermal infrared). We deploy two upstream lightweight You Only Look Once (YOLO11n) detectors (2.6 million parameters, 6.5 giga-floating-point operations (GFLOPs) at $640 \times 640$ resolution): one trained on multi-environment RGB sequences and one on multi-environment thermal sequences, using batch size 16 in single-precision floating-point (FP32) arithmetic for numerical stability on embedded avionics. Frame confidences are integrated via soft disjunctive late fusion and filtered across a causal five-frame sliding window ($625\text{ ms}$ latency bound at $8\text{ Hz}$). We evaluate five post-processing strategies: baseline raw thresholding, five-frame moving average, five-frame median filtering, five-frame history consensus, and a learned pure-PyTorch Mamba selective state-space model (SSSM), with decision thresholds optimized systematically on validation sequences. Evaluations across synthetic SAR video benchmarks demonstrate that causal temporal filtering effectively suppresses environmental false alarms while preserving prompt target sensitivity.
 \end{abstract}
 
