 Downstream, false-alarm reduction directly benefits autonomous aerial operations. On bandwidth-limited IoT links (e.g., satellite links operating at 2.4--10\,kbps), suppressing spurious alarms prevents telemetry queue congestion and packet loss \cite{kyrkou2021emergencynet}. Furthermore, because each alarm mandates a 15--30\,s UAV loiter maneuver consuming $P_{\text{hover}} \approx 280\text{ W}$, eliminating dozens of false alarms conserves tens of kilojoules of battery reserves per sortie, directly expanding operational flight endurance and search coverage.
 
+Table~\ref{tab:operational_impact} models the environment-stratified operational savings achieved by each causal post-processing filter relative to baseline raw thresholding (M1). For each method $m$ and environment $e \in \{\text{Desert}, \text{Forest}\}$, the number of false positive alarms avoided is quantified as $\Delta \text{FP}_m(e) = \text{FP}_{\text{baseline}}(e) - \text{FP}_m(e)$. Feeding $\Delta \text{FP}_m(e)$ into Eq.~\eqref{eq:bandwidth_waste}, the total uplink telemetry bandwidth preserved is $\Delta \Omega_m(e) = \Delta \text{FP}_m(e) \cdot S_{\text{pkt}}$, evaluated for standard telemetry packets ($S_{\text{pkt}} = 1.2\text{ kB}$) and visual thumbnail verification packets ($S_{\text{pkt}} = 45\text{ kB}$). Using Eq.~\eqref{eq:energy_loiter}, the cumulative battery energy conserved by averting unneeded loiter confirmation maneuvers is $\Delta E_m(e) = \Delta \text{FP}_m(e) \cdot P_{\text{hover}} \cdot T_{\text{loiter}}$, where $P_{\text{hover}} \approx 280\text{ W}$ and loiter verification duration $T_{\text{loiter}} \in [15, 30]\text{ s}$. The corresponding extension in search sortie endurance is projected directly as $\Delta t_{\text{flight}} = \Delta E_m(e) / P_{\text{hover}} = \Delta \text{FP}_m(e) \cdot T_{\text{loiter}}$.
+
+\subsection{Limitations}
+\label{sec:limitations}
+
+While the comparative framework provides systematic insights into causal temporal post-processing for aerial SAR, several operational and experimental constraints bound the scope of the present findings:
+\begin{enumerate}
+    \item \textbf{Sample Size of Test Split:} The evaluation is conducted across 12 held-out test video sequences (2 clips per scenario per environment, totaling 960 decimated test frames). Although sequence-level partitioning strictly prevents temporal data leakage, broader multi-hour flight campaigns are required for comprehensive statistical characterization.
+    \item \textbf{Biome Diversity:} The study is constrained to two representative wilderness biomes (arid desert and temperate forest). Maritime, alpine snowpack, wetland, and urban-wildland interface environments introduce distinct thermal-optical scattering profiles not captured in this corpus.
+    \item \textbf{Flight Regime and Dynamics:} Video sequences model a constrained aerial flight regime ($30$--$50$\,m above ground level at flight speeds of $8$--$15$\,m/s). High-speed turbulent sorties or low-altitude hover-scan maneuvers may alter apparent target ground sampling distance and jitter profiles.
+    \item \textbf{Atmospheric and Weather Phenomena:} All clips reflect nominal daytime operating conditions; severe weather degradations including dense advection fog, heavy precipitation, and blowing dust are unmodeled.
+    \item \textbf{Single-Target Assumption:} The current formulation focuses on individual person search scenarios and does not incorporate multi-target identity disambiguation or crowded group spatial tracking.
+    \item \textbf{Absence of Physical Hardware-in-the-Loop Validation:} Algorithmic latencies and memory bounds are derived theoretically and benchmarked in software; physical deployment aboard an integrated flight avionics stack (e.g., NVIDIA Jetson companion computer interfacing with a Pixhawk flight controller) remains unvalidated in this pass.
+\end{enumerate}
+
 \section{Conclusion}
 \label{sec:conclusion}
 
-This paper investigated the integration of lightweight multimodal person detection with causal temporal post-processing for UAV wilderness search and rescue. By coupling visible RGB and thermal infrared streams via soft disjunctive late fusion, target cues are preserved under single-sensor dropouts. We deployed YOLO11n (2.6M parameters, 6.5 GFLOPs at $640 \times 640$) trained with batch size 16 in FP32 precision as our edge detection baseline. Downstream, five causal post-processing methods operating across a five-frame ($625\text{ ms}$) sliding window were benchmarked under validation-optimized decision thresholds. Causal temporal post-processing effectively filters transient environmental noise spikes while maintaining rapid target confirmation, conserving telemetry bandwidth and onboard battery reserves.
+This paper investigated the integration of lightweight multimodal person detection with causal temporal post-processing for UAV wilderness search and rescue across two environments (arid desert and temperate forest) and two visual modalities (visible RGB and thermal infrared). We deployed two separate YOLO11n models (2.6M parameters, 6.5 GFLOPs at $640 \times 640$) trained with batch size 16 in FP32 precision as our edge detection baselines, combined via soft disjunctive late fusion. Downstream, five causal post-processing methods operating across a five-frame ($625\text{ ms}$) sliding window were benchmarked under validation-optimized decision thresholds. Causal temporal post-processing effectively filters transient environmental noise spikes across modalities and terrains while maintaining rapid target confirmation, conserving telemetry bandwidth and onboard battery reserves.
 
 Future work will focus on hardware-in-the-loop flight testing on NVIDIA Jetson companion computers aboard physical UAVs, spatio-temporal multi-target tracking, and domain adaptation to unmodeled weather conditions such as fog and rain.
 
